         hmm. i have just remembered response files exist (https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/109979#issuecomment-1958353504). maybe that would be better, so that the linker options are always still shown? and —verbose would print the contents of the response file but otherwise have no effect? Aren't the response files deleted even after a failed link? possibly! also using a response file for some arguments but not others seems complicated lol > Aren't the response files deleted even after a failed link?

yes, you are correct. they use a tempdir that's autoremoved unless you use -C save-temps. https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/fe9f2722a04346c1aa7cba8ca49169242393fdc1/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/back/link.rs#L105

we're the compiler though, so we have access to that file before it's deleted. so --verbose could still work. > using a response file for some arguments but not others seems complicated lol

oh god ok so i looked into how much work this would be and the answer is. a lot. we do not store the linker arguments in a Vec, we put them directly into a Command, and the order of the arguments is extremely important. so either we would need a separate fake Command that we pass into all the Linker implementations, or we'd need to refactor the trait so it only gives a Vec<String> instead of a command. both of those sound annoying and i would rather not do that here, i do not think in practice it will be common to need to see the linker arguments *and* be unable to pass --verbose. Looks like the following is enough to avoid object files from showing up while printing the rest. It is easy to extend this to rlibs too. I'm going to try to see if I can adapt it to get a good balance between compactness and showing useful information.

<details><summary>The diff</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/back/link.rs b/compiler/rustc_codegen_ssa/src/back/link.rs
index 487ef3f88a8..45fafd2321a 100644
