Is this actually true? As written it's not relying on any magic semantics? The only reason I can think of is if you want to be able to better match it for optimisation, but this is a relatively boring idiom that's easy to match? Yeah fair, this comment can probably also be removed. @nikic having *just* `with_addr` as an intrinsic in LLVM should not cause the problems you mentioned regarding address capture vs provenance capture, right? Would such an intrinsic be worth it, or are we compiling the current `with_addr` well enough? I think it's good enough for now. LLVM still understands the provenance of the pointer, and will not really be able to do anything with the address in either case (unless either the with_addr idiom optimizes away due to equal provenance, or is converted to integer, both of which are supported). I think the only problem with the current representation is the `self.addr()` call (which currently implies an escape), but that will be solved once we have a proper representation for that. This is no longer true now, right? Ah, good catch. ```suggestion
//!   end of one allocated object is the "same" address as the start of another allocated object,
``` ```suggestion
//! The Original Pointer for an allocated object has provenance that constrains the *spatial*
``` ```suggestion
//!   from the same allocated object. Provenance is used to say what exactly "derived from" even
``` ```suggestion
//! * Spatial: A set of bytes that the pointer is allowed to access.
```

provenance may gain the ability to access multiple non-adjacent ranges e.g. to handle memory `mmap`-ed in multiple locations or for things like address masking where the CPU ignores some pointer bits (such as AArch64 TBI which ignores the most significant 8 bits). I've chosen a slightly different wording, since "set of bytes" to me sounds like a set containing byte-sized values. ```suggestion
//! * Temporal: The timespan during which the pointer is allowed to access those memory addresses.
``` I wonder if this should be something more generic such as "(access) type" or "permissions", since I imagine we would also want to include executable? Converting a data pointer to a function pointer doesn't necessarily give a validly callable function pointer? Rust is not a Von Neumann machine, so the notion of *memory* being "executable" doesn't even make sense for us. Fair enough - I was just thinking of something [like the following](https://godbolt.org/z/E4c8G4GM7):
```rust
pub fn call_into_jit(jit_code_buffer: *const i32) {
    let code: extern "C" fn() = unsafe { std::mem::transmute(jit_code_buffer) };
    (code)();
}
```
But that is most likely an unrelated issue since things like mmap() MMU permissions fall outside of the Rust abstract machine. Oh yeah, JITs are way outside the base Rust mode and require some advanced surgery -- basically, on the level of inline assembly. Sounds good, please feel free to mark this as resolved :) I'm being surprised by the degree to which people are analyzing these comments in other venues, so I'm commenting on them here.

In what sense could this be more efficient? If you mean compile time, it would be nice to indicate that specifically. If you are referring to the fact that this needs to call `self.addr()` and therefore currently lowers to a `ptrtoint`, I'd prefer that be commented-on in the `addr` implementation or in cg_ssa instead, because the efficiency problem is in LLVM specifically. Another thing worth commenting on is the relative prevalence of this function specifically. So, if this is slow to compile, but is only present a couple of times in a code base, that's not as big of a deal.

It's definitely going to be hard to gauge these things until it's stable and more widely used, though. I am referring to the fact that this does pointer arithmetic, which is completely unnecessary. LLVM will likely optimize the arithmetic away, but it's still silly. > If you are referring to the fact that this needs to call self.addr() and therefore currently lowers to a ptrtoint, I'd prefer that be commented-on in the addr implementation or in cg_ssa instead, because the efficiency problem is in LLVM specifically.

That could be a comment in codegen, not in lib -- lib is doing everything right here by using `transmute` instead of `as`.

Not specific to this PR, but I'm curious: is it necessary to set the preferred dark theme given that it says to ignore the system theme and manually specifies the current theme? Seems somewhat unfortunate to enable `--generate-link-to-definition` for *all* GUI tests. I guess it could have a `compile-flags` comment like compiletest does, but that would be a larger change. Why is this change being made? Not sure it is, but just in case... :) Actually I think I'll enable it only for one test to simplify things. In some very rare cases, the `click` command just below was run before the content was there I think, making the `click` fail because of the missing element. Could you instead add a new test called something like `link_to_definition` and enable `--generate-link-to-definition` only for that? Enabling `--generate-link-to-definition` for `test_docs` in particular is surprising because there's nothing in the name that would make you think that. But why did you make the change in this PR? Could you open a separate PR for this change since it seems to be unrelated? Ok, that seems fine 👍  Ok! There's no dependency on `implementors` in this `Cargo.toml`. I think you can just remove the `implementors` crate since it's not necessary for what we're testing? It'd probably be a good idea to add a check that there are any links at all, just in case the bootstrap check isn't working. Oh damn, I added too much without paying attention, removed it! It fails if no element matches the given selector. :) Ah, that makes sense 👍  Could you add a FIXME here that says this?

```rust
// FIXME: implement a `// compile-flags` command or similar
//        instead of hard-coding this test
``` Could you make these fields public to add more test coverage?

Just dropping by without having any context and without having read the rest of this patch to say that you probably need to add some extra code to differentiate between `tymethod` and `method` to generate correct URLs for required associated functions (in traits). It seems redundant with the existing [`ItemType::from(tcx.def_kind(original_def_id))`](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/bf3c6c5bed498f41ad815641319a1ad9bcecb8e8/src/librustdoc/formats/item_type.rs#L116).

That function is less complete than this one, but I'm pretty sure that function is just incomplete. Nice to see this factored out into its own function! :+1: Why does root_path need to be set in order for doc(hidden) to be relevant here? This is a good test case, but it could use a bunch more:

* One that covers a method call on a trait (this is where `tymethod`, that @fmease mentioned, comes in.

  ```rust
  use std::io::Read;

  pub fn bar<T: Read>(readable: T) {
    // @has - '//a[@href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/trait.Read.html#tymethod.read"]' 'read'
    let _ = readable.read(&mut []);
  }
  ```

* One covering the module case:

  ```rust
  // @has - //a[@href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/std/io/index.html"]' 'std::io'
  use std::io;
  ```

* One covering an extern fn:

  ```rust
  use libc::exit;
  pub fn extern_call() {
    // @has - '//a[@href="https://docs.rs/libc/latest/libc/fn.exit.html"]' 'exit'
    exit();
  }
  ```

* One covering an enum variant:

  ```rust
  pub fn extern_call() {
    // @has - //a[@href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/option/enum.Option.html#variant.Some"]' 'Some'
    let _ = Some(0);
  }
  ```

  * One covering a macro call:

    ```rust
    pub fn extern_call() -> Result<(), ()> {
      // @has - //a[@href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/nightly/core/macro.try.html"]' 'try'
      try!(Err(());
      Ok(())
    }
    ```

... and probably several others, that I'm not thinking of. The differences between them come from the way they use `#`-anchors, the index.html file that modules use, the way `#[macro_export]` is rewritten to be at the top level, the way `extern` blocks are the parent of extern functions but have no path, and other strange differences between "real" Rust paths and the paths Rustdoc uses.
 Is there a reason why `generate_item_def_id_path` can't be used all the time? Because `root_path` is only set for the "jump to def" feature and the `effective_visibilities.is_public()` method returns `false` if the item isn't in the cache (which is the case most of the time). Might be nice to add a comment explaining that indeed.  Good point. I'll add more tests. Because if a local item is not in the cache, something really strange is going on and I'm not sure whether or not we should try to get more information for it. Indeed, I should re-use the existing. Added the comment. So the existing was not enough because for methods, it needs information about the parent (if it's a trait, then it's not a method but a `TyMethod`. I added your examples and one more: unit struct construction. Allowed to uncover an interesting case with `DefKind::Ctor`. Just double-checked, the other reason is that the code expects local items to be allows "known" so we only use `extern_locations` to get information about the (foreign) crate containing the item. I understand that `extern_locations` points at third-party websites, so that links to the standard library and within docs.rs work. I also understand why `external_paths` is separate from locally defined `paths`.

I'm wondering why `external_paths` needs to exist at all. If it needs to be cached for performance, why the map can't be lazily populated on use using this same code? Currently, it's populated by a mess of calls to `record_extern_fqn`, which could potentially be removed in favor of relying on the compiler more. For now `external_paths` only contains foreign items exposed in the public API. It allows to prevent recomputing this path every time, so I think it's worth it to keep it. Now I suppose the question is: should we store foreign paths for "jump to def" items? The problem is that we don't have perf checks for this feature, so it' be complicated to track exactly the impact. What do you think about opening an issue to cache these paths and also update the rust perf tool to include a crate which uses this feature? Could external_paths be computed using this same logic instead of the mess of calls to record_foreign_fqn that are used now? I suppose it's possible. Want me to do it here or in a follow-up? I'd like to do it as a commit on this PR. The idea is that all the existing test cases should wind up covering this code since it'll be exercised more. After trying to figure out how to do it: f we don't have `record_extern_fqn`, first it means that `exact_paths` won't be filled anymore. It also means we need to update the `Cache` when it's not mutable anymore (and stored inside a `Rc<>` in `SharedContext`), which means we need to put it into a `RefCell`. With this approach, we need to add a method which would take a callback (because you can't return a value of `RefCell` from a method). But then that makes a lot of parts of the code much more tricky to handle... ```suggestion
// @has - '//a[@href="{{channel}}/core/sync/atomic/struct.AtomicIsize.html"]' 'std::sync::atomic::AtomicIsize'
``` ditto et seqq. ... It's so obvious I'm ashamed I didn't think about it myself...

