issue_text:
Tracking issue: #118117

Public API:
```rust
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct FormattingOptions { … }
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum Sign {
    Plus, 
    Minus
}
#[derive(Copy, Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub enum DebugAsHex {
    Lower,
    Upper
}

impl FormattingOptions {
    pub fn new() -> Self;
    pub fn sign(&mut self, sign: Option<Sign>) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn sign_aware_zero_pad(&mut self, sign_aware_zero_pad: bool) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn alternate(&mut self, alternate: bool) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn fill(&mut self, fill: char) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn align(&mut self, alignment: Option<Alignment>) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn width(&mut self, width: Option<usize>) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn precision(&mut self, precision: Option<usize>) -> &mut Self;
    pub fn debug_as_hex(&mut self, debug_as_hex: Option<DebugAsHex>) -> &mut Self;

    pub fn get_sign(&self) -> Option<Sign>;
    pub fn get_sign_aware_zero_pad(&self) -> bool;
    pub fn get_alternate(&self) -> bool;
    pub fn get_fill(&self) -> char;
    pub fn get_align(&self) -> Option<Alignment>;
    pub fn get_width(&self) -> Option<usize>;
    pub fn get_precision(&self) -> Option<usize>;
    pub fn get_debug_as_hex(&self) -> Option<DebugAsHex>;

    pub fn create_formatter<'a>(self, write: &'a mut (dyn Write + 'a)) -> Formatter<'a>;
    }

impl<'a> Formatter<'a> {
    pub fn new(write: &'a mut (dyn Write + 'a), options: FormattingOptions) -> Self;
    pub fn with_options<'b>(&'b mut self, options: FormattingOptions) -> Formatter<'b>;
    pub fn sign(&self) -> Option<Sign>;

    pub fn options(&self) -> FormattingOptions;
}
```

Relevant changes from the public API in the tracking issue (I'm leaving out some stuff I consider obvious mistakes, like missing `#[derive(..)]`s and `pub` specifiers):

- `enum DebugAsHex`/`FormattingOptions::debug_as_hex`/`FormattingOptions::get_debug_as_hex`: To support `{:x?}` as well as `{:X?}`. I had completely missed these options in the ACP. I'm open for any and all bikeshedding, not married to the name.
- `fill`/`get_fill` now takes/returns `char` instead of `Option<char>`. This simply mirrors what `Formatter::fill` returns (with default being `' '`).
- Changed `zero_pad`/`get_zero_pad` to `sign_aware_zero_pad`/`get_sign_aware_zero_pad`. This also mirrors `Formatter::sign_aware_zero_pad`. While I'm not a fan of this quite verbose name, I do believe that having the interface of `Formatter` and `FormattingOptions` be compatible is more important.
- For the same reason, renamed `alignment`/`get_alignment` to `aling`/`get_align`.
- Deviating from my initial idea, `Formatter::with_options` returns a `Formatter` which has the lifetime of the `self` reference as its generic lifetime parameter (in the original API spec, the generic lifetime of the returned `Formatter` was the generic lifetime used by `self` instead). Otherwise, one could construct two `Formatter`s that both mutably borrow the same underlying buffer, which would be unsound. This solution still has performance benefits over simply using `Formatter::new`, so I believe it is worthwhile to keep this method.

review_comments:
Ah, this one's a relatively small change but this is much better. We capitalize the R in Rust in e.g. `repr(Rust)` for the same reason we capitalize the C in `extern "C"`, but I think it's more consistent to just discard:
```suggestion
lint_improper_ctypes_unsized_box = this box for an unsized type contains metadata, which makes it incompatible with a C pointer
lint_improper_ctypes_unsized_ptr = this pointer to an unsized type contains metadata, which makes it incompatible with a C pointer
lint_improper_ctypes_unsized_ref = this reference to an unsized type contains metadata, which makes it incompatible with a C pointer
``` typo:

```suggestion
lint_improper_ctypes_sized_ptr_to_unsafe_type =
    this reference (`{$ty}`) can be translated into a C pointer, but `{$inner_ty}` itself is not FFI-safe
```

Hmm, maybe this?
```suggestion
    this reference (`{$ty}`) is ABI-compatible with a C pointer, but `{$inner_ty}` itself does not have a C layout
``` Elsewhere we refer to this as having a "definite size", so maybe this could be `Sizedness::Definite`? This is the sizedness associated with `extern type`, right? I think we should be specific here, so maybe `Sizedness::UnsizedExternType`? can be sounds slightly off. must be, should be?

anyways, at least one typo:
```suggestion
                    // so the fact that a pointer's pointee should be treated as opaque to one side or the other can be explicitly written out.
``` There is no need to use a parenthetical aside when you stopped the prior sentence.
```suggestion
                    // (`extern "C" fn fn_name(...) {...}`) versus declarations (`extern "C" {fn fn_name(...);}`).
                    // This is touched upon in https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/66220
                    // and https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/72700
``` nothing requires us to linebreak exactly every 100 characters, so it'd be nice if this was broken up a little more clearly. also we should probably use the required-in-2024 `unsafe` prefix to the extern block:
```suggestion
                    // there's a nuance on what this lint should do for
                    // function definitions: `extern "C" fn fn_name(...) {...}`
                    // versus declarations: `unsafe extern "C" { fn fn_name(...); }`.
``` I'm not sure this logic actually holds, given another Rust program can declare these within `extern "C" { }` instead of `use`. ...right, I need to reread this in the morning. Why are we bailing if `base_ty` is not a mutable pointer? Shouldn't `*const CStr` also get that treatment? ```suggestion
            ty => {
``` I think I was just imitating [a place where this clause exists](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/blob/c44b3d50fea96a3e0417e8264c16ea21a0a3fca2/compiler/rustc_lint/src/types.rs#L924).
I have no idea why it is there, but I can only assume it was well thought out by someone or other.

guess I'll go down the git-blame - look up pull - look up issue again, my bad for not doing so earlier, like I did for the other "weird" clauses I had to copy around in my changes. I'll reply here once rather than once per comment on that commented explanation.

Yeah, the explanation I used here was very wishy-washy. Thankfully I have a better one now.
Namely, the compiler (very likely?) needs you to have non-opaque types in the function header if you are going to use them in a non-opaque way in the function body) I think I'll go with `Sizedness::UnsizedWithExternType` if you don't see a problem with it.
Because it's not just for extern types but also structs whose last member('s last member)* is an extern type. wait I think I know.
this special casing of `*const` only applies for the help message of:
```
lint_improper_ctypes_cstr_help =
    consider passing a `*const std::ffi::c_char` instead, and use `CStr::as_ptr()`
```
and writing to the output of that function is UB.

what we would need instead is a different piece of advice for how to pass mutable strings through C-ABI boundaries. (: thanks! done okay! yes, better keep the terminology consistent done done Ah, okay. typo
```suggestion
                // FfiResult stack to an ImproperCTypesLayer Vec
``` ```suggestion
                            if let Some(layer) = cimproper_layers.last_mut() {
                                layer.inner_ty = Some(ty.clone());
                            }
``` ```suggestion
                            if let Some(layer) = cimproper_layers.last_mut() {
                                layer.inner_ty = Some(ty.clone());
                            }
``` I believe we can skip always cloning the `last_ty` this way. Now it's, er, just the last `ty`.
```suggestion
                // should always have at least one type
                let last_ty = cimproper_layers.last().unwrap().ty.clone();
``` correct me if I am wrong but `inner_ty` is always the `ty` of the next item, making this field technically superfluous? we just need to deal in 2-item windows.

granted it does seem like it is a pain in the ass for the diagnostic infra if we don't have both available, so this should be fine. It's my preference that we use the control flow type for, er, control flow, if only to disambiguate from "left empty for later".
```suggestion
                let mut ffiresult_recursor = ControlFlow::Continue(&ffir);
``` ```suggestion
                            ffiresult_recursor = ControlFlow::Break(());
``` It's my preference that we use the control flow type for, er, control flow, if only to disambiguate:
```suggestion
                while let ControlFlow::Continue(ref ffir_rec) = ffiresult_recursor {
```

It would be better to return the original span unformatted since returning an `Err` will prevent formatting whatever this pattern is in, e.g a match expression.
```suggestion
            PatKind::Guard(..) =>  Ok(context.snippet(self.span).to_owned()),
```

Also, could you please add a test case annotated with `#![feature(guard_patterns)]` in `src/tools/rustfmt/tests/target` so that we can keep track of this on the rustfmt side.
 I had understood that the rustfmt policy was "don't format new syntax in any way whatsoever when implementing it in rustc, let us handle that". Has this changed/did I misunderstand? Should the test be correctly formatted or incorrectly formatted? When it comes to the policy it's more about implementing formatting rules. We don't consider returning the span unformatted as implementing any formatting. That said, there are cases where the formatting is simple / obvious and I think in those cases it's fine to implement things directly in rustc for example #132915. 

As for the test case, I think it should be formatted based on what the current expectations are, and I think it's okay if that changes once t-style has a chance to codify rules. Maybe we could just reuse the example from the RFC?

```rust
match user.subscription_plan() {
    (Plan::Regular if user.credit() >= 100) | (Plan::Premium if user.credit() >= 80) => {
        // Complete the transaction.
    }
    _ => {
        // The user doesn't have enough credit, return an error message.
    }
}
```
 Sounds good, ty! How does the last commit look? Things look good on the rustfmt side  ty! this might not be right in all contexts as parentheses might be required. Please leave a fixme so we can track that. I don't think this should allow guards to be parsed? This is an irrefutable context would this change macro behavior, allowing guard patterns when they are previously not accepted as a `$a:pat` before? Add a test for that.  Despite the context being irrefutable, I believe it's better to parse them and emit a refutability error later. Doing it that way makes it easier to get a good error message (rather than just "unexpected token if..."), and prevents the situation where adding parentheses around the pattern substantially changes the error message.

It's also implied [by the RFC text](https://rust-lang.github.io/rfcs/3637-guard-patterns.html#in-assignment-like-contexts) that only `if let` and `while let` will be changed to use `PatternNoTopGuard`. should I just popen/pclose around it then? better to be correct with too many parens. If/when we allow `if let` pattern guards, then `x if let y = f(x)` is an example of an irrefutable pattern guard. Even if we don't allow it I expect users might would them, e.g.:
```rust
macro_rules! deref {
    ($p: pat) => { ref x if let $p = Deref::deref(x) }
}
fn foo(slice: &[Box<u32>]) {
    for deref!(x) in slice {
        // user expects x: &u32
    }
}
``` done Seems okay to me, since we're not trying to be rustfmt here

