This stability attr is redundant, see c1a29b30d157689caafa34238e87313cda0fccc5 and apply a similar diff. Hm, remind me, are structs ever passed in registers in the Xtensa ABI? Some ABIs do, others do not.

Which registers is this notionally saving in the case of, say, the no-initial-args case (for `extern "C" fn(...)`, valid in all C implementations that adopt C23 and previously available as an extension in some)?

The reason I'm asking is because I want to know if this is one of those varargs ABIs where `extern "C" fn (c_int, ...)` where only 1 arg, a `c_int`, is passed, is ABI-equivalent to `extern "C" fn (c_int, c_int)` on entry into the actual function. Some ABIs deliberately design their varargs implementation to work like that, allowing you to basically deal with those two function pointers interchangeably. Others do not allow such because all varargs are handled in a distinct way so you only would match ABI details between varargs and other functions with only fixed args by accident.

( Obviously this also requires the varargs function itself to correctly "unpack" its arguments from the `va_list`. ) The ordering of these two statements seems reversed as it says "depends" but leaves the dependency relationship ambiguous. When reversed, explaining the dependency relationship naturally comes as a consequence of being able to refer back to the data layout that va_ndx is an index into.

I believe this is correct? Feel free to rephrase further.
```suggestion
    // The first 24 bytes (equivalent to 6 registers) come from va_reg, the rest from va_stk.
    // Thus if va_ndx is less than 24, the next va_arg *may* read from va_reg,
    // otherwise it must come from va_stk.
``` Fun. Yes, structs can be passed via registers, structs can even be split across registers and stack space, _but_ primitive types will never be split. For instance, a `u128` either has to be all in registers, or all in stack never split across both.

> The reason I'm asking is because I want to know if this is one of those varargs ABIs where extern "C" fn (c_int, ...) where only 1 arg, a c_int, is passed, is ABI-equivalent to extern "C" fn (c_int, c_int) on entry into the actual function. Some ABIs deliberately design their varargs implementation to work like that, allowing you to basically deal with those two function pointers interchangeably. Others do not allow such because all varargs are handled in a distinct way so you only would match ABI details between varargs and other functions with only fixed args by accident.

From the Xtensa ISA:

![image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f87fd0ec-0156-4e20-bd78-a97055c2e5e7)

So I believe in this case calling a normal function or vararg function is the same, but like you mentioned the function then has to unpack it's arguments in the vararg case.
 This comment is incorrect according to your answer to my question, it should at least briefly mention the struct vs. primitive detail. For many C ABIs, the handling of a struct can differ from an equivalent set of scalar types, so we should not leave this ambiguous. We have at least two senses of "offset" in play, offsets into the va_list struct itself, and offsets into the varargs area. Let's be excruciatingly clear.
```suggestion
    let va_reg_offset = 4;
